-1.0180214643478394]]}, {"t": " somewhat", "lp": -0.4952453374862671, "top": [[" somewhat", -0.4952453374862671], [" relatively", -1.8289704322814941]]}, {"t": " **", "lp": -0.7470700144767761, "top": [[" **", -0.7470700144767761], [" low", -1.7813793420791626]]}, {"t": "experimental", "lp": -1.4934296607971191, "top": [["experimental", -1.4934296607971191], ["driver", -1.6504464149475098]]}, {"t": " and", "lp": -0.39726653695106506, "top": [[" and", -0.39726653695106506], ["/", -2.2592928409576416]]}, {"t": " version", "lp": -0.42007797956466675, "top": [[" version", -0.42007797956466675], [" driver", -1.21868097782135]]}, {"t": "-dependent", "lp": -0.05425357446074486, "top": [["-dependent", -0.05425357446074486], ["-sensitive", -3.2043275833129883]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are distributed across the GPU's compute engines. This is set via the `xe` kernel module parameter (e.g., `modprobe xe ccs_mode=1`).\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode 0 (default)**\n- CCS contexts are distributed across **all** CCS engines in a round-robin fashion.\n- This is the standard behavior and works well for most workloads.\n\n**ccs_mode 1**\n- CCS contexts are pinned to a **single CCS engine** for the lifetime of the context.\n- A context created on one CCS stays on that CCS until it's destroyed.\n- This can reduce context-switching overhead when a workload consistently uses the same engine.\n\n**ccs_mode 2**\n- CCS contexts are distributed across CCS engines but with **different affinity/pinning semantics** than mode 1.\n- Historically this mode has had some stability concerns on certain driver versions.\n\n## When to use mode 4\n\n**ccs_mode 4** is the mode you'd want for **multi-GPU / MxGPU virtualization scenarios**. It's designed for configurations where CCS contexts need to be handled in a way that supports virtualized or partitioned GPU setups.\n\n## Practical guidance\n\nFor a **single A770 in a desktop/workstation**, you almost always want to leave this at the **default (mode 0)** unless you're doing specific performance tuning or debugging. The modes are primarily relevant for:\n\n- **Server/datacenter deployments**\n- **Virtualization** (SR-IOV, MxGPU)\n- **Targeted performance investigation**\n\n## A note of caution\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter is somewhat **experimental and version-dependent"}